@page "/Projects/ChunkSystemRefactor"
<div class="ProjectPage">
  <DefaultProjectHeading CurrentProjectURL="Projects/LoadingScreen" />
  <div class="ProjectDescription">
    <div>
      <h3>Project description</h3>
      <span>
        This might be the biggest refactor I've ever done (with over 560 changed files, 26,887 additions and 42,427
        deletions).
        The chunk system was the first system I wrote at Birungi Studio. I'm quite proud of it.
        It's a very contained system as I used assembly definition files.
        I wrote it in a way that the actual difference wasn't noticeable for my designer.
        The "APIs" of the system stayed the same so to say.
        Except for the backend of course. I improved and recoupled the code wherever I thought necessary.
      </span>
    </div>
    <h3>Decoupling</h3>
    <span>
      The original chunk system was kind of hard to read through. With over 50+ fields between two classes with all the
      logic in it.
      I concluded that reading through all the dependencies and mapping them before I went to work wasn't worth it.
      So I wrapped the whole system in an assembly definition. This way I could instantly see that there were a lot of
      code that depended
      on the system.
      I replaced those dependencies with NotImplementedExceptions or changed them entirely if I saw a quick solution for
      it.
    </span>
    <h3>The refactor plan</h3>
    <span>
      Since the original system wasn't exactly S.O.L.I.D. I decided to start with a more single responsibility approach.
      I also split the internal logic from the "API" of the system. The API of the system was also the part where the
      logic got its data from.
      So it ended up looking something like this:
    </span>
    <img src="/images/projects/refactorchunksystem/mermaid-diagram-2026-04-20-203749.png" />
    <span>
      That makes ChunkLogic.Components the API part and ChunkLogic the internal part.
    </span>
    <h3>Automation of ChunkData gathering</h3>
    <span>
      The old system existed of a ScriptableObject (which is a Unity data object) and a difficulty script (per
      difficulty you needed one). Both needed
      to be filled out manually.
      I reduced it to one manual field: the
      biomes. (On the left mine on the right the originals)
    </span>
    <div class="ImageGrid">
      <img class="first" src="/images/projects/refactorchunksystem/ChunkDataAPI.png" />
      <img class="second" src="/images/projects/refactorchunksystem/OldChunkDataManual.png" />
    </div>
    <span>
      When run my system now outputs something like this:
    </span>
    <img src="/images/projects/refactorchunksystem/ChunkDataOutput.png" />
    <h3>The ChunkPool class</h3>
    <span>
      One of the flaws of the previous system was that the instantiation of the chunks was compositionally done through
      the ChunkLogic component and not async. This meant that the whole system wasn't batch loadable or awaitable. The
      new ChunkPool loader was written with both of those things in mind:
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@SetupChunkPool" />
    <span>
      It is now fully async and batch loadable. It also has a callback for when it's done loading so other systems can
      react to it.
    </span>
    <h3>Collider Trimmer</h3>
    <span>
      A bit later in time I decided to take a look at the farm chunk prefabs.
      My designer had complained about FPS drops when the game entered the farm biome.
      When I toggled the collider visibility I wasn't surprised anymore (all the green objects are colliders).
    </span>
    <img src="/images/projects/refactorchunksystem/BeforeColliderTrim.png"/>
    <span>
      So I asked my designer if I was allowed to edit the chunks and when I got the green flag on that I went to work.
      At first I tried fixing them up manually. 
      Shortly after I realised that that wasn't worth my time. 
      I would be better off writing <NavLink href="/Projects/ColliderTrimmerScript">an automation script for it</NavLink> (the script is too big to nicely fit in the site).
      Once automated, it took me only a few seconds to permanently fix this for the game. 
      Instead of what would've been three days and my sanity...
      Here is a preview of what the system did to the same but trimmed by my system:
    </span>
    <img src="/images/projects/refactorchunksystem/AfterColliderTrim.png" />
  </div>
</div>

@code
{
  string SetupChunkPool = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["SetupChunkPool"];
}